## The Connector Portal System

The key technical idea in NovaSpire Unity 1.3 is the **Connector Portal
System**.

The connector acts as the bridge between the Mother AI and a designed
agent. It defines how the Mother AI connects to that agent, what the
agent is allowed to do, and how information flows between the two.

The portal system makes this connection **flexible and replaceable**.

This creates a **hotswap architecture**, where agents can be connected,
disconnected, upgraded, replaced, or reorganized without breaking the
entire system.

The connector portal can define:

- which agent is being activated
- what task the agent is designed for
- what tools the agent can use
- what rules the agent must follow
- what data it can access
- what output it should return
- when it should disconnect
- how it reports back to the Mother AI

This makes the whole AI workspace cleaner, safer, and easier to scale.

---

## Why Hotswap Matters

In a normal messy AI workspace, tools and agents can become tangled
together. Files are reused without structure. Old agents remain
active. Experimental systems get mixed with production systems. It
becomes hard to know what is important, what is outdated, and what is
safe to use.

**Hotswap** solves this by making agents modular.

Instead of rebuilding the entire AI system every time something
changes, NovaSpire Unity 1.3 allows the Mother AI to change its active
toolset through connector portals.

This means the system can:

- add new agents
- remove unused agents
- upgrade existing agents
- test new modules
- isolate broken systems
- clean old workspace structures
- keep the main AI stable

The Mother AI remains consistent, while the agent layer can evolve.

